> This post is part of the [BOLF Blogvember challenge](https://blog.spacehey.com/entry?id=2251917). Today, the word minimum is **25 words**.

Election day in Michigan is today, and as I was researching my candidates for the coming election, I came across [this campaign video](https://www.facebook.com/watch/?v=1370046751907300) posted by John James, Republican gubernatorial candidate. To save you the anguish of having to click on a Facebook link, I'll tell you what happened in the clip.

James' Democrat opponent, Jocelyn Benson, was given an interview question: whether "biological males should be allowed in girls' sports". Jocelyn takes a moment to respond, and when they do, they reluctantly say that sporting organizations should be charge of that decision. When pressed further, they say that "the entity that should be deciding that... is not the government."

Disregarding the obviously-manipulative framing of the question(and the fact that transgender people have [no proven advantage](https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41633803/) over cisgender people in sports), what really struck me about this video is that Benson didn't... really say anything! She didn't actually make a case for or against transgender people, even though that was really the point of the question. The presumed reason she would dodge the question is because transgender people are a hot-button topic at the moment... but that's exactly why she should've responded!

---

In order to be an effective politician, you need to make your positions known. Your constituents(or potential constituents) need to know what you stand for, and why. Increasingly, though(barring some notable exceptions), it seems that many politicians refuse to address any politicized issues! If you're asked to speak about transgender people, then it should be your prerogative to show your support(or condemnation) for transgender people.

As we see the [Overton window](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overton_window) shift to the right over time, it's the civic responsibility of an individual to be vocal about their values, *especially* as many of these "political" issues involve the erosion of human and civil liberties. We're in an unprecedented time in history, and to act otherwise is to be complacent. To treat things as "normal" is to legitimize the hateful rhetoric that's permeated today's partisan politics. The only valid response to this hateful rhetoric is a thorough and direct condemnation; nothing else may suffice.

---

I often think about Martin Luther King, Jr.'s [Letter from a Birmingham Jail](https://www.africa.upenn.edu/Articles_Gen/Letter_Birmingham.html), especially the part where he expresses his disappointment with the "white moderate". He describes these moderates as those who prefer negative peace(or the absence of tension) over positive peace(or the presence of justice); they are the people who superficially support change, but not the means to create it. Today, the situation is even worse than Martin Luther King, Jr. described: the moderates of today not only support the status quo through inaction, but they are complicit in the backsliding of the progress we have made over the past half-century or so.

Something's got to give!